Fluid Mechanics — Richard Fitzpatrick (@fd-fitzpatrick: 0, is #reference, #part-of @fd-sources) { r_075CGXNA8WHV26ZW53C7XTQ48V
Richard Fitzpatrick, University of Texas at Austin. [Complete public course notes](https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/336L/336l.html) · [Linked analytical text](https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/336L/Fluid/Fluidhtml.html) · [Separate published book](https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/books/Fluids/Fluids.html).

Read the complete analytical notes and mathematical appendices through the advanced sequence. They support hydrostatics, interfaces, potential and viscous flows, boundary layers, waves, tides and compressible flow. Use the exercises to derive results without relying on empirical correlations alone.

The public notes have 2016-dated pages. The author's book page identifies Theoretical Fluid Mechanics as an IOP publication dated December 2018; it is not the same artifact as the free HTML notes. The course deliberately emphasizes classical Newtonian models and exact analytical treatments. No blanket CC licence is claimed for the author-hosted text. Retain the source distinction and follow credited notices.

**Return:** [Fluid dynamics sources — books, courses, methods and physical evidence](../Sources.lingua#r_3Y3TVV9VQAB50M2ZSECK5JTYVG).
